   "chapter": 6,
   "chapter_title": "CHAPTER VI. Pig and Pepper"
  },
  "b012-1-1": {
   "kind": "branch",
   "text": "\"Then the Dormouse shall!\" they cried out together. \"Wake up, Dormouse!\" And they pinched it on both sides at once.\n\nThe Dormouse slowly opened its eyes. \"I wasn't asleep,\" it said in a hoarse, feeble voice: \"I heard every word you fellows were saying.\"\n\n\"But you never answered Alice's question,\" said the Hatter severely, as though this were entirely the Dormouse's fault and none of his own. \"What *does* happen when you come to the beginning again?\"\n\n\"That's exactly what I want to know,\" said Alice, feeling rather bolder now that the question had been taken up by someone else. \"It seems to me you never wash the things at all, only push them along, and so of course they never *do* come to the beginning, because there isn't one, because it's a circle, and a circle hasn't got a beginning any more than a riddle has an answer—\"\n\n\"Who said a riddle hadn't an answer?\" said the March Hare.\n\n\"You did,\" said Alice; \"at least, you couldn't tell me the answer to yours, so it comes to the same thing.\"\n\n\"Not knowing the answer isn't the same as there not being one,\" said the Hatter, with great dignity. \"It's very likely there's a beautiful answer, only nobody's ever thought of it yet.\"\n\n\"Then it isn't really an answer at all,\" Alice persisted, \"it's only a sort of an answer-shaped hole where an answer ought to be.\"\n\n\"I never heard such nonsense in all my life!\" cried the March Hare.\n\n\"You're one to talk of nonsense,\" said the Hatter, \"you who can't tell your right paw from a corkscrew.\"\n\n\"I can so!\"\n\n\"You can NOT!\"\n\n\"I say you can't, and what's more, I never wanted to know why a raven is like a writing-desk, I only asked it to be polite!\"\n\n\"Polite!\" said the Dormouse scornfully, and fell asleep again in the middle of the word.\n\nAlice found herself quite forgotten in the dispute, which grew louder and crosser every moment, until the very sugar-tongs seemed to be taking sides; and she began to wish, rather wistfully, that she had never mentioned beginnings at all.",
   "summary": "Alice presses the Hatter and March Hare about their unanswered riddle, and the tea-table dissolves into a bitter, circular squabble that leaves her forgotten and wishing she'd never brought it up.",
   "choices": [
    {
     "label": "Continue",
     "to": "b012-1-2"
    }
   ],
   "branch_id": "b012-1"
  },
  "b012-1-2": {
   "kind": "branch",
   "text": "\"But it *is* nonsense,\" Alice said, quite firmly, for she had had a good deal of practice at being firm with kittens, and found it came in useful with March Hares as well. \"You keep on saying there's an answer, only nobody's found it, but that's exactly what you'd say if there wasn't one at all, so I don't see how it proves anything either way.\"\n\n\"It proves,\" said the Hatter, drawing himself up until his hat nearly touched the branch above him, \"that you have a very disagreeable way of asking questions.\"\n\n\"I didn't ask anything just then,\" said Alice.\n\n\"You were *about* to,\" said the March Hare, \"which is worse, because at least a question that's been asked is over and done with, whereas one that's only about to be asked might go on being about-to for ever, and *that's* a great deal more wasteful of Time than anything *I've* ever done to him.\"\n\n\"You haven't done anything to him at all,\" said the Hatter, \"except quarrel with him at the Queen's concert, and get left at six o'clock for your trouble.\"\n\n\"I did NOT get left,\" said the March Hare, growing pink about the ears. \"I *chose* to stay.\"\n\n\"At tea-time, for ever?\"\n\n\"It suits me very well, thank you.\"\n\n\"It doesn't suit the teacups,\" muttered Alice, half to herself, for she had noticed that several of them had not been washed at all, but only turned upside down and called clean, which she privately considered a kind of cheating, though she did not quite like to say so out loud a second time.\n\n\"Somebody's been putting ideas into her head,\" said the Hatter darkly, looking at the Dormouse, who was by now fast asleep again and could not possibly have put anything into anybody's head, having, as far as Alice could tell, quite given up the business of thinking altogether.\n\n\"Nobody's put anything anywhere,\" said Alice, growing a little cross herself. \"I only asked a simple question, and instead of answering it you've all gone and quarrelled, which I think is very poor manners, considering it's your own tea-party and not mine.\"\n\nThere was rather an uncomfortable silence after this, broken only by the sugar-tongs, which went on clicking crossly at nobody in particular.",
   "summary": "The squabble between the Hatter and March Hare escalates into pointed personal jabs about wasted time and bad manners, while Alice grows increasingly frustrated at being ignored, and the scene ends in an awkward, sulky silence at the table.",
   "choices": [
    {
     "label": "Continue",
     "to": "b012-1-3"
    }
   ],
   "branch_id": "b012-1"
  },
  "b012-1-3": {
   "kind": "branch",
   "text": "\"Once upon a time there were three little sisters,\" the Dormouse began, in a great hurry, as if he wished to get the words out before he fell asleep again, which seemed on the whole rather likely, \"and their names were Elsie, and Lacie, and Tillie, and they lived at the bottom of a well—\"\n\n\"What did they live on?\" said Alice, who always took a great interest in questions of eating and drinking.\n\n\"Treacle,\" said the Dormouse, after thinking a minute or two.\n\n\"They couldn't have done that, you know,\" Alice gently remarked; \"they'd have been ill.\"\n\n\"So they were,\" said the Dormouse; \"*very* ill,\" and here the Hatter said that if Alice couldn't be civil she had better finish the story herself, which struck Alice as a very odd sort of punishment, seeing that finishing stories was precisely the thing she most wanted to do; and before she could say so, the whole table got up and moved one place on, so that the Dormouse was in the Hatter's place, and Alice found herself a good deal worse off than before, having only the March Hare's spilt milk to look at instead of a plate.\n\n\"I don't see how you can go on living at the bottom of a well and drawing treacle at the same time,\" she said, rather sharply, and the Hatter told her, quite crossly, that if she couldn't take an interest in the story she might leave it alone altogether, upon which Alice got up in great disgust and walked off, looking back once to see them trying to put the Dormouse into the teapot, head first.\n\n\"At any rate I'll never go *there* again,\" she said, picking her way through the wood; and it was while she was still saying it that she noticed a little door in one of the trees, and, going through it, found herself once more in the long low hall, with the little glass table close by.\n\nThis time she managed it beautifully, unlocking the door with the little golden key, and nibbling at the mushroom until she was small enough to pass through, and so, at last, she stepped out into the loveliest garden she had ever seen, all bright flower-beds and cool fountains—only, just inside the gate, stood a large rose-tree with white roses on it, and three gardeners, all shaped rather like flat oblong cards, were busily painting them red.\n\n\"Would you tell me, please,\" said Alice, a little timidly, \"why you are painting those roses?\"\n\n\"Why the fact is, you see, Miss,\" said Five, \"this here ought to have been a *red* rose-tree, and we put a white one in by mistake.\"\n\nBefore Two could answer, there came a great shout from the far end of the garden, of \"The Queen! The Queen!\" and the three gardeners flung themselves flat upon their faces, and Alice, hearing the tramp of many feet, looked round eagerly to see the procession.\n\nIt was, on the whole, the grandest thing Alice had ever seen out of a picture-book: soldiers shaped like the gardeners, ornamented all over with clubs and diamonds and hearts and spades; then ten courtiers, all covered with white rabbits' fur (this puzzled Alice a good deal, though she had no time to say so); then the royal children, holding hands and jumping about in pairs; then the guests, mostly Kings and Queens, among whom Alice recognized the White Rabbit himself, talking very fast with an anxious smile; and last of all, THE KING AND QUEEN OF HEARTS.\n\nThe procession stopped opposite Alice, and the Queen said severely, \"Who is this?\" and, receiving no answer but a shrug from the Knave, cried, \"Idiot!\" and turned to Alice. \"What's your name, child?\"\n\n\"My name is Alice, so please your Majesty,\" said Alice very politely; but she added, to herself, \"Why, they're only a pack of cards, after all. I needn't be afraid of them!\"\n\nThe Queen turned crimson with fury, glared at her a moment, and then screamed, \"Off with her head! Off—\"\n\n\"Nonsense!\" said Alice, very loudly and decidedly, and the Queen was silent.\n\nThe King laid his hand upon her arm, and timidly said, \"Consider, my dear: she is only a child!\" and the Queen, still frowning, turned upon the three gardeners lying flat upon the ground.\n\n\"What have you been doing here?\" she demanded, and Two, in a low voice, began explaining about the roses; whereupon the Queen shrieked again, \"Off with their heads!\" and Alice, without stopping to think, walked quietly over to a large flower-pot standing near, and popped the three gardeners into it, one on top of another, where they lay quite still, and by the time the soldiers came to look for the culprits, there were none to be found, which fact the Queen did not trouble herself about, having a great many other heads to think of ordering off.\n\n\"Can you play croquet?\" the Queen shouted; and Alice found herself walking beside the White Rabbit into the middle of the ground, where she was handed a live flamingo for a mallet, and told to make a hedgehog serve for the ball, and soldiers doubled themselves over to make the arches, and the whole game was such a chaos of shrieking hedgehogs and flapping flamingoes and quarrelling players that Alice never got even one hedgehog through one arch before the Queen was in a furious passion, and stamping about, and screaming, \"Off with his head!\" or \"Off with her head!\" about once in a minute.\n\nAlice was just wondering, with her flamingo tucked awkwardly under one arm, how she was ever to get away without being rude, when she noticed a curious appearance in the air; it puzzled her very much at first, but, after watching it a minute or two, she made it out to be a grin, and she said to herself, \"It's the Cheshire Cat: now I shall have somebody to talk to.\"\n\n\"How are you getting on?\" said the Cat, as soon as there was mouth enough for it to speak with, and Alice was in the middle of telling it, quite frankly, that she thought they none of them played at all fairly, and that they all quarrelled so dreadfully one couldn't hear oneself speak, and that they didn't seem to have any rules in particular, when the King, coming up behind her, noticed the Cat's head and did not like the look of it at all.\n\n\"I don't like the look of it,\" he said; \"however, it may kiss my hand, if it likes.\"\n\n\"I'd rather not,\" the Cat remarked, at which the King grew scarlet, and appealed to the Queen, who settled every difficulty, great or small, by saying, \"Off with his head!\"\n\n\"I'll fetch the executioner myself,\" said the King eagerly, and hurried off; and the executioner's argument was, that you couldn't cut off a head unless there was a body to cut it off from, and that he'd never had to do such a thing before, and wasn't going to begin at his time of life; and the King's argument was, that anything that had a head could be beheaded, and that the Cat had better not talk nonsense; and the Queen's argument was, that if something wasn't done in less than no time, she'd have everybody executed, all round, which was the reason they were all so unhappy about it.\n\nAlice could think of nothing else to say but, \"It belongs to the Duchess: you'd better ask her about it.\"\n\n\"She's in prison,\" the Queen said to the executioner. \"Fetch her here.\" And the executioner went off like an arrow, and by the time he came back, the Cat's head had faded away entirely, and, by the time the Duchess arrived, breathless, and rubbing her hands together, there was no cat to be seen anywhere at all.",
   "summary": "The tea-party dissolves into further quarrels and rudeness as the Dormouse's unfinished treacle-well story annoys everyone, prompting Alice to storm off, find the door in the tree, shrink back down, and enter the beautiful garden, where she encounters the card gardeners, the royal procession, the chaotic croquet game, and the Cheshire Cat's disputed beheading, ending just as the Cat vanishes before the fetched Duchess arrives.",
   "choices": [
