    }
   ],
   "chapter": 8,
   "chapter_title": "CHAPTER VIII. The Queen’s Croquet-Ground"
  },
  "b015-1-1": {
   "kind": "branch",
   "text": "Alice thought this a very good opportunity for slipping away unnoticed (which, considering that the whole court was occupied in either running about after an invisible head, or being run about after by a Queen who had never in her life learned to stand still), and so, without so much as a \"by your leave,\" she edged herself sideways behind the nearest rose-bush, and was pleased to find that nobody so much as looked up.\n\n\"If I were a grin without a cat,\" she said to herself, picking her way through the bushes, which had a most inconvenient habit of leaning just where she wished to walk, \"I should want to be somewhere quiet, and think things over. Not that a grin can think, exactly—at least I don't see how it could, having no head to think *with*—but then it managed to argue with the King and the Queen and the executioner all at once, and that's more than *I* could do without any head at all, so perhaps I am wrong about it.\"\n\nShe had got a good way in among the roses now—the white ones, all splashed crossly with red paint, as though someone had been careless with the jam—and the sound of the Queen's voice, still shouting about executions, had grown pleasantly faint and far away, like a wasp that has decided to bother somebody else's picnic instead.\n\n\"Puss?\" she called softly, feeling rather foolish, for it is not easy to call politely for something that may not have any ears to hear you with. \"Cheshire Puss, are you there?\"\n\nThere was no answer, but a rose just beside her twitched, all by itself, in a manner that no rose has any business twitching in, and a very faint voice—if it *was* a voice, and not merely the wind agreeing with itself—seemed to say, quite close to her ear:\n\n\"That depends a good deal on what you mean by *there*.\"",
   "summary": "Alice slips away from the chaotic court into the rose-bushes to search for the Cheshire Cat, and hears its disembodied, riddling voice answer her call.",
   "choices": [
    {
     "label": "Continue",
     "to": "b015-1-2"
    }
   ],
   "branch_id": "b015-1"
  },
  "b015-1-2": {
   "kind": "branch",
   "text": "Alice looked all round the rose-bush, and then all round the other side of it, and then underneath, where a snail was going about its business in a manner that suggested it had no opinion on the matter whatsoever, and still she could not see anything that looked even a little bit like a cat, grinning or otherwise.\n\n\"Well,\" she said, rather crossly, for it is very trying to be philosophical at by a voice that will not show itself, \"I mean *there*, of course—as in, in that particular place, wherever it happens to be, which I should think was a perfectly ordinary thing to mean, and not a bit puzzling.\"\n\n\"Ah,\" said the voice, and this time Alice was nearly sure it came from a gap between two roses where the paint had dripped into a little red puddle, \"but you see, a *place* is only a *there* to somebody who is standing in a *here*, and as I haven't got anywhere in particular to stand *with*, being at present only a Grin and not the least bit of a Cat, I can't properly be said to be anywhere at all. Though I will say,\" it added, sounding pleased with itself, \"that not being anywhere is a good deal more comfortable than being somewhere and having one's head talked about by a King.\"\n\n\"I should think it would be,\" said Alice, feeling that this at least made a kind of sense, though she could not have explained how. \"But if you're not anywhere, how am I to find you? It seems very unfair, to be asked to look for something and told beforehand that it can't be looked *at*.\"\n\n\"You're not looking for the Cat,\" said the voice, and here it gave what Alice felt sure, without being able to say why, was a wink, \"you're looking for the *Grin*, and a Grin, my dear, is a thing you don't so much look *for* as look *with*—\" and at this the puddle of paint seemed to widen ever so slightly at both corners, in a manner that put Alice unmistakably in mind of a smile.",
   "summary": "Alice searches among the rose-bushes for the Cheshire Cat and finds only its disembodied, riddling voice, which explains—in maddening Wonderland logic—that it cannot properly be \"somewhere\" while it is only a Grin, though its presence begins to reveal itself in the red paint splashed on the roses.",
   "choices": [
    {
     "label": "Continue",
     "to": "b015-1-3"
    }
   ],
   "branch_id": "b015-1"
  },
  "b015-1-3": {
   "kind": "branch",
   "text": "And that, thought Alice, watching the paint widen and widen until it was really quite a respectable Grin, and then, by degrees, dwindling again into nothing more than a stain on a white rose, was very likely the last she should see of it, and she was just wondering whether one could be said to have *lost* a thing that had never properly *been there* to be lost, when a voice behind her said, \"A moral, my dear, in everything, if only one takes the trouble to dig it out,\" and she found her arm most firmly linked in the arm of the Duchess, who smelt strongly of pepper and moralising and had, apparently, been waiting behind a gooseberry bush for exactly this purpose.\n\nThe Duchess had got no further than \"and the moral of *that* is—\" when the Queen's voice came booming up the path like a hundred trumpets all disagreeing with each other, and the Duchess vanished so suddenly and so completely that Alice half suspected she had learnt the trick from the Cat.\n\nAfter that the croquet went on worse than ever, the mallets wandering off in the middle of strokes and the Queen sentencing nearly everybody to execution in a voice that grew hoarser and crosser with every \"Off with—!\", though Alice noticed, when she thought to look, that nobody's head actually came off, the King having a quiet, busy sort of way of pardoning people the instant the Queen's back was turned.\n\nBy and by the Queen grew tired even of this, and, taking Alice by the hand rather more kindly than Alice had expected, brought her to a curious creature asleep in the sun, which the Queen called a Gryphon and left with the single instruction that it should show the young lady the Mock Turtle, and hear his history—and it was the Gryphon, in the end, walking beside her down to a rocky little ledge where a very melancholy creature sat sighing as though his heart, or whatever a Mock Turtle keeps instead, would break, who brought her, after a great deal of schoolroom talk and a good deal of dancing and a whiting explaining itself and one thing leading, as things will, to another, to the moment where they both wanted, very particularly, to hear her repeat something.",
   "summary": "Alice loses the vanishing Grin, is swept off by the moralising Duchess who flees at the Queen's approach, and the story hastens through the pardoned executions, the Gryphon, and the Mock Turtle's introduction, arriving at the point where they ask Alice to recite something.",
   "choices": [
    {
     "label": "Continue",
     "to": "c019"
    }
   ],
   "branch_id": "b015-1"
  },
  "b015-2-1": {
   "kind": "branch",
   "text": "Alice thought this a very good opportunity for finding out something she had been wondering about ever since she had first heard of the Duchess being shut up, and, without giving herself time to consider whether it was quite proper to interrupt a King in the middle of his searching, she walked straight up to him and dropped a small curtsey, which she felt was only polite, even to a King who was at that moment down on his hands and knees peering under a rose-bush.\n\n\"If you please, your Majesty,\" said Alice, \"I should like to know why the Duchess was put in prison at all. It seems very hard on her, when she hadn't a head to lose in the first place—I mean, when it wasn't _her_ head that was in question.\"\n\nThe King started up so suddenly that his crown nearly fell off, and had to be caught by the Knave, who happened to be passing with a tray of tarts he had no business to be carrying. \"Why—that is—\" said the King, growing quite pale, and looking about him as if he hoped the answer might be written somewhere on the grass, \"the reasons of State, you know, are not always to be—that is—a King cannot always—\"\n\n\"It was for boxing the Queen's ears!\" said a shrill voice at Alice's elbow, and there was the Queen herself, grown suddenly very red in the face, though whether from the sun or from temper Alice could not tell, and rather thought it must be both. \"Boxing the Queen's ears is high treason, and everybody knows it! Why, I've had people executed for looking at me sideways, and this child stands there asking impertinent questions as if it were a perfectly reasonable thing to do!\"\n\n\"It seems to me a very reasonable thing to do,\" said Alice, before she could stop herself, \"when nobody else will explain anything properly,\" and she folded her hands behind her back and waited, feeling rather braver than she had any right to feel, considering everything.",
   "summary": "Alice confronts the flustered King about the Duchess's imprisonment, prompting the Queen to reveal the reason (boxing her ears) and grow furious at Alice's boldness.",
   "choices": [
    {
     "label": "Continue",
     "to": "b015-2-2"
    }
   ],
   "branch_id": "b015-2"
  },
  "b015-2-2": {
   "kind": "branch",
   "text": "The Queen's face, which had been red before, now went a colour that Alice felt sure was not to be found in any of her paint-boxes at home—a sort of purplish-crimson, like a beetroot that had been left too long in the sun, or perhaps like the Duchess's own nose, which Alice remembered had been remarkably large. \"Off with—\" began the Queen, and then stopped, rather to Alice's surprise, and looked almost put out about it, as if she had used up her supply of heads to take off for the morning and would have to wait for a fresh consignment.\n\n\"You cannot go on saying that,\" said Alice, growing bolder still, for she had noticed that nothing whatever happened when the Queen said it, which seemed to prove that it was mostly talk, \"about everybody's head, I mean, because there are only so many heads in this garden, and if you took them all off there would be nobody left to play croquet with, or to plant the roses, or to do anything at all, and then you would be a Queen of nobody, which doesn't sound very grand.\"\n\nThis seemed to strike the King as an excellent point, for he nodded so hard that his crown really did fall off this time, and rolled away under the rose-bush he had lately been searching, so that he had to go down on his hands and knees again to fetch it, muttering all the while something about arithmetic being a very good argument if only people would attend to it.\n\n\"The Duchess,\" said the Queen, recovering herself with an effort, and speaking now in the particular voice grown-up people use when they mean to end a conversation whether it is finished or not, \"boxed my ears at the last croquet-party, over a matter of a hedgehog, and there's an end of it. Prisons are for people who box ears, and that is all the reason anybody needs, or is going to get.\"\n\nAnd with that she turned on her heel, and went off across the ground to see about a flamingo that had wandered, quite of its own accord, into the middle of a gooseberry bush.",
   "summary": "Alice presses the King and Queen for the real reason behind the Duchess's imprisonment, and the flustered Queen finally admits it was for boxing her ears over a hedgehog before storming off.",
   "choices": [
    {
     "label": "Continue",
     "to": "b015-2-3"
    }
   ],
   "branch_id": "b015-2"
  },
  "b015-2-3": {
   "kind": "branch",
   "text": "The Duchess came bustling up almost before the Queen was out of sight, looking a good deal less alarming out of prison than Alice had expected, and tucked her arm through Alice's as though they had been the greatest of friends for years and years. \"Everything's got a moral, if only you can find it,\" said the Duchess, pressing so close that her sharp little chin dug quite painfully into Alice's shoulder, and she went on to prove it, finding a moral in the mustard, in the flamingoes, in the very hedgehogs, until Alice's head fairly spun with morals. But the Queen came striding back just then, red in the face and impatient about her game, and said only, \"Now, Duchess! Either your moral or your head, and quickly!\"—at which the Duchess turned quite pale under her paint and vanished among the rose-bushes faster than Alice would have believed possible for anybody so stout.\n\nAfter that the croquet went on worse than ever, hedgehogs unrolling and wandering off just when they were wanted, flamingoes twisting round to stare reproachfully, and the Queen shouting \"Off with his head!\" or \"Off with her head!\" at nearly everybody in turn, until Alice grew quite tired of counting; though she noticed, with some relief, that the executions never seemed actually to happen, the soldiers having a private understanding with the King, who pardoned each victim the moment the Queen's back was turned.\n\nBy and by the Queen, quite out of breath, asked Alice if she had seen the Mock Turtle yet, and upon Alice confessing she did not so much as know what a Mock Turtle was, carried her off to a great sulky-looking creature asleep in the sun, which the Queen called a Gryphon, and told it to take the little girl to see the Mock Turtle and hear his history. So the Gryphon led her over the rocks to where a Mock Turtle sat, sighing as if his heart would break, and presently, between his sobs, he began telling her of the school he had gone to at the bottom of the sea, under an old Turtle they used to call Tortoise—\n\n\"Why did you call him Tortoise, if he wasn't one?\" Alice asked.\n\n\"We called him Tortoise because he taught us,\" said the Mock Turtle angrily.",
   "summary": "The Duchess moralizes at Alice until the Queen frightens her away; Alice rejoins the chaotic, secretly bloodless croquet game, then is taken by the Queen to the Gryphon, who leads her to the sorrowful Mock Turtle, who begins recounting his undersea schooling.",
   "choices": [
    {
     "label": "Continue",
     "to": "c019"
    }
   ],
   "branch_id": "b015-2"
  },
  "c015": {
   "kind": "canon",
   "text": "The chief difficulty Alice found at first was in managing her flamingo:\nshe succeeded in getting its body tucked away, comfortably enough,\nunder her arm, with its legs hanging down, but generally, just as she\nhad got its neck nicely straightened out, and was going to give the\nhedgehog a blow with its head, it _would_ twist itself round and look\nup in her face, with such a puzzled expression that she could not help\nbursting out laughing: and when she had got its head down, and was\ngoing to begin again, it was very provoking to find that the hedgehog\nhad unrolled itself, and was in the act of crawling away: besides all\nthis, there was generally a ridge or furrow in the way wherever she\nwanted to send the hedgehog to, and, as the doubled-up soldiers were\nalways getting up and walking off to other parts of the ground, Alice\nsoon came to the conclusion that it was a very difficult game indeed.\n\nThe players all played at once without waiting for turns, quarrelling\nall the while, and fighting for the hedgehogs; and in a very short time\nthe Queen was in a furious passion, and went stamping about, and\nshouting “Off with his head!” or “Off with her head!” about once in a\nminute.\n\nAlice began to feel very uneasy: to be sure, she had not as yet had any\ndispute with the Queen, but she knew that it might happen any minute,\n“and then,” thought she, “what would become of me? They’re dreadfully\nfond of beheading people here; the great wonder is, that there’s any\none left alive!”\n\nShe was looking about for some way of escape, and wondering whether she\ncould get away without being seen, when she noticed a curious\nappearance in the air: it puzzled her very much at first, but, after\nwatching it a minute or two, she made it out to be a grin, and she said\nto herself “It’s the Cheshire Cat: now I shall have somebody to talk\nto.”\n\n“How are you getting on?” said the Cat, as soon as there was mouth\nenough for it to speak with.\n\nAlice waited till the eyes appeared, and then nodded. “It’s no use\nspeaking to it,” she thought, “till its ears have come, or at least one\nof them.” In another minute the whole head appeared, and then Alice put\ndown her flamingo, and began an account of the game, feeling very glad\nshe had someone to listen to her. The Cat seemed to think that there\nwas enough of it now in sight, and no more of it appeared.\n\n“I don’t think they play at all fairly,” Alice began, in rather a\ncomplaining tone, “and they all quarrel so dreadfully one can’t hear\noneself speak—and they don’t seem to have any rules in particular; at\nleast, if there are, nobody attends to them—and you’ve no idea how\nconfusing it is all the things being alive; for instance, there’s the\narch I’ve got to go through next walking about at the other end of the\nground—and I should have croqueted the Queen’s hedgehog just now, only\nit ran away when it saw mine coming!”\n\n“How do you like the Queen?” said the Cat in a low voice.\n\n“Not at all,” said Alice: “she’s so extremely—” Just then she noticed\nthat the Queen was close behind her, listening: so she went on,\n“—likely to win, that it’s hardly worth while finishing the game.”\n\nThe Queen smiled and passed on.\n\n“Who _are_ you talking to?” said the King, going up to Alice, and\nlooking at the Cat’s head with great curiosity.\n\n“It’s a friend of mine—a Cheshire Cat,” said Alice: “allow me to\nintroduce it.”\n\n“I don’t like the look of it at all,” said the King: “however, it may\nkiss my hand if it likes.”\n\n“I’d rather not,” the Cat remarked.\n\n“Don’t be impertinent,” said the King, “and don’t look at me like\nthat!” He got behind Alice as he spoke.\n\n“A cat may look at a king,” said Alice. “I’ve read that in some book,\nbut I don’t remember where.”\n\n“Well, it must be removed,” said the King very decidedly, and he called\nthe Queen, who was passing at the moment, “My dear! I wish you would\nhave this cat removed!”\n\nThe Queen had only one way of settling all difficulties, great or\nsmall. “Off with his head!” she said, without even looking round.\n\n“I’ll fetch the executioner myself,” said the King eagerly, and he\nhurried off.\n\nAlice thought she might as well go back, and see how the game was going\non, as she heard the Queen’s voice in the distance, screaming with\npassion. She had already heard her sentence three of the players to be\nexecuted for having missed their turns, and she did not like the look\nof things at all, as the game was in such confusion that she never knew\nwhether it was her turn or not. So she went in search of her hedgehog.\n\nThe hedgehog was engaged in a fight with another hedgehog, which seemed\nto Alice an excellent opportunity for croqueting one of them with the\nother: the only difficulty was, that her flamingo was gone across to\nthe other side of the garden, where Alice could see it trying in a\nhelpless sort of way to fly up into a tree.\n\nBy the time she had caught the flamingo and brought it back, the fight\nwas over, and both the hedgehogs were out of sight: “but it doesn’t\nmatter much,” thought Alice, “as all the arches are gone from this side\nof the ground.” So she tucked it away under her arm, that it might not\nescape again, and went back for a little more conversation with her\nfriend.\n\nWhen she got back to the Cheshire Cat, she was surprised to find quite\na large crowd collected round it: there was a dispute going on between\nthe executioner, the King, and the Queen, who were all talking at once,\nwhile all the rest were quite silent, and looked very uncomfortable.\n\nThe moment Alice appeared, she was appealed to by all three to settle\nthe question, and they repeated their arguments to her, though, as they\nall spoke at once, she found it very hard indeed to make out exactly\nwhat they said.\n\nThe executioner’s argument was, that you couldn’t cut off a head unless\nthere was a body to cut it off from: that he had never had to do such a\nthing before, and he wasn’t going to begin at _his_ time of life.\n\nThe King’s argument was, that anything that had a head could be\nbeheaded, and that you weren’t to talk nonsense.\n\nThe Queen’s argument was, that if something wasn’t done about it in\nless than no time she’d have everybody executed, all round. (It was\nthis last remark that had made the whole party look so grave and\nanxious.)\n\nAlice could think of nothing else to say but “It belongs to the\nDuchess: you’d better ask _her_ about it.”\n\n“She’s in prison,” the Queen said to the executioner: “fetch her here.”\nAnd the executioner went off like an arrow.\n\nThe Cat’s head began fading away the moment he was gone, and, by the\ntime he had come back with the Duchess, it had entirely disappeared; so\nthe King and the executioner ran wildly up and down looking for it,\nwhile the rest of the party went back to the game.",
   "summary": "Alice struggles through the chaotic croquet game with her flamingo mallet and hedgehog ball while the Queen furiously orders beheadings for minor infractions. The Cheshire Cat's floating head appears and chats with Alice, provoking a dispute when the King dislikes it and orders it removed, leading to a bizarre argument between the King, Queen, and executioner over how to behead a headless-bodied cat. The Duchess is sent for to resolve the issue, but the Cat vanishes entirely before she arrives.",
   "choices": [
    {
     "label": "Slip away to hunt for the vanished Cheshire Cat",
     "to": "b015-1-1"
    },
    {
     "label": "Approach the King to ask about the Duchess's sentence",
     "to": "b015-2-1"
    },
